{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And one who steals a man and sells him.</b> Why is this said? Because it is said ״כִּי יִמָּצֵא אִישׁ גֹּנֵב נֶפֶשׁ מֵאֶחָיו״ (Deuteronomy 24) — I have only a man who stole a person; a woman, or a tumtum, or an androgyne who stole — from where? Scripture says ״וְגֹנֵב אִישׁ וּמְכָרוֹ.״ And since it says here ״וְגֹנֵב אִישׁ,״ I have only one who steals a man; one who steals a woman — from where? Scripture says ״גֹּנֵב נֶפֶשׁ.״ Therefore both were necessary: what this one omits, that one reveals (Sanhedrin there)." "<b>And he is found in his hand.</b> That witnesses saw him steal him and sell him, and he was already found in his hand before the sale (there)." "<b>He shall surely be put to death.</b> By strangulation; every death penalty stated in the Torah without specification is strangulation. (And the topic was interrupted and it wrote ״וְגֹנֵב אִישׁ״ between ״one who strikes his father and his mother״ and ״one who curses his father,״ and it appears to me this is the dispute: one master holds we link striking to cursing, and one master holds we do not link (there)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:36.998418865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}